    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>SystÃ¨me de cote ÃnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Your house is quite leaky at 9 ACH which is responsible for 29% of the houses heat loss. The fresh air for the furnace going into the crawlspace may be a big part of that. Installing an electronic damper may alleviate some of that. The chimney damper is another significant leak. Switching to electric heat would eliminate the need for fresh air for the combustion process. Other air leaks include the attic hatch and other ceiling penetrations (light fixtures). Before you add insulation to the attic try and locate and seal these. The stove fan and dryer vent leak- check and or replace the exterior hoods. There were, also leaks at each of the cable penetrations, including where the conduit for the electrical panel penetrates the wall and through the outlets and light switches on exterior walls.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>I added 4 inches of rigid foam insulation (R-20) to the outside of your walls as a potential upgrade. This reduced the heat loss through the walls by 55%.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>The ceiling is a major source (19%) of the house heat loss. As an upgrade I doubled the amount of insulation in the attic and added 2 inches of rigid foam insulation to the underside of the ceiling, as David had mentioned. This reduced the heat loss through the attic by 60%.</ceilings>
                <foundation>The crawlspace is a fairly largge area of heat loss so I added 1.5 inches of rigid foam insulation to the floor. Using high density spray foam, instead, could also be a barrier against air and moisture leakage and Radon gas. For some reason the program is not registering the change in the report and doesnt show the reduction in heat loss.</foundation>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
